<p>Architecture is the art form you live inside. Unlike painting or sculpture, a building has a practical function: it must shelter, contain, or organize human activity. But architecture becomes art when it does more than function. When a building shapes your experience of space, light, and movement in a way that reveals something about the values of the people who built it, it is doing what all art does.</p>
<p>Unit 6 introduces four requirements that every work of architecture must meet: technical (it must stand), functional (it must serve its purpose), spatial (it must organize space meaningfully), and revelatory (it must say something about human values). The unit then examines two broad traditions: earth-rooted architecture, which emphasizes connection to the ground and site, and sky-oriented architecture, which reaches upward and defies gravity.</p>
<p>Pay attention to the buildings this unit discusses. You have walked through buildings your entire life without thinking about what they are doing to you. This unit changes that.</p>
<div class="callout callout--note"><span class="callout__icon" aria-hidden="true">ℹ️</span><div class="callout__content">
<p>Learning Objectives</p>
<p>After completing this unit, you should be able to:</p>
<ul>
<li>Identify the four requirements of architecture: technical, functional, spatial, and revelatory</li>
<li>Distinguish between earth-rooted and sky-oriented architecture</li>
<li>Explain key structural concepts: post-and-lintel, arch, cantilever, dome, flying buttress</li>
<li>Describe how architecture uses space, light, and materials to reveal values</li>
<li>Analyze a specific building using the framework from the lesson</li>
</ul>




